# Act One

= A clerk discovers that the harbour lamps have been counting something.

FADE IN:

EXT. HARBOUR WALL - DUSK #1#

Wind drags salt across a line of iron lanterns. Each one carries a brass counter, and every counter shows a different number.

PELL (28), a customs clerk with ink on both cuffs, walks the wall with a ledger. She stops at the fourth lantern and frowns.

PELL
(reading)
Four hundred and twelve. Yesterday it was four hundred and eleven.

She taps the glass. The counter ticks to four hundred and thirteen.

PELL (CONT'D)
That is not how lamps work.

[[The counters will matter in scene 3; keep the numbers consistent.]]

A gull lands on the lantern, looks at Pell, and leaves. /* An earlier draft
had two gulls. They were cut. */

CUT TO:

INT. CUSTOMS HOUSE - RECORDS ROOM - NIGHT #2#

Shelves of ledgers climb into darkness. A single oil lamp burns on a desk. OSKAR (60s), the night registrar, polishes his spectacles without looking up.

OSKAR
You are late, and you are wet.

PELL
The lanterns on the wall are counting.

OSKAR
Lanterns do not count. Clerks count. That is the arrangement.

PELL
(setting the ledger down)
Then explain the fourth lantern.

Oskar opens the ledger. His finger travels down the column of figures and stops. He is quiet for a long moment.

OSKAR
(softly)
Four hundred and thirteen.

PELL
It ticked while I watched it.

OSKAR
Every lantern on the wall was fitted in the year of the flood. Nobody has opened one since. The counters were meant to be decorative.

PELL
Decorative things do not go up by one every night.

OSKAR
They do if somebody winds them.

A long pause. Somewhere above them, a shelf creaks.

OSKAR (CONT'D)
Or if something passes them.

Pell picks up the lamp from the desk. Oskar puts a hand over the flame and lowers it back down.

OSKAR (CONT'D)
Not tonight. Tonight you dry off and write down exactly what you saw. In ink. Tomorrow we go together and we open the fourth lantern, and we count whatever is inside it, and we write that down too.

PELL
And if it is empty?

OSKAR
Then we have a different problem, and I would rather meet that one in daylight.

!OSKAR'S LAMP gutters. He steadies it.

PELL
Fine. Daylight.

OSKAR ^
Daylight.

They look at each other. Neither of them is reassured.

~ Count the lamps and count the tide,
~ count the ones that stayed outside.

> END OF ACT ONE <

===

# Act Two

.CELLAR UNDER THE FOURTH LANTERN - DAWN #3#

A narrow stone stair ends at an iron hatch. Pell and Oskar crouch before it with a crowbar and a lamp.

OSKAR
On three.

PELL
On three.

The hatch opens. Inside, stacked to the roof, are small grey stones, each marked with a chalk tally. Hundreds of them. Pell starts counting under her breath.

Oskar reaches past her and picks up the nearest stone. The chalk reads: 413.

OSKAR (V.O.)
We did not finish counting that day.

> FADE OUT.
